1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
They called it flying the ball. Others called it crazy.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Enclosed in a small rotating plastic sphere with two .50 caliber machine guns,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
these gunners defended the B-17s and B-24s from enemy fighter attack.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
They were small in size but big on bravery.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
The Ball Turret Gunners, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The American bomber crews who flew raids against Nazi Germany from 1942 to 1945 knew, all too well, the dangers they faced.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,960
From their air bases in England, the men of the Army Air Force attacked heavily defended targets deep inside enemy territory in broad daylight.

8
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:19,760
Once they started on a bomb run, they had to fly straight and level in order to drop their deadly cargo on target.

9
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This made them sitting ducks easy prey for German fighter planes and anti-aircraft gunners.

10
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Tens of thousands of airmen perished in the skies over Europe.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The entire United States Marine Corps didn't suffer as many casualties in the war against

12
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:26,420
Japan as did the airmen who flew bombers over Germany.

13
00:02:26,421 --> 00:02:32,420
They lived on an emotional roller coaster, safe at their air base on one day, in mortal

14
00:02:32,421 --> 00:02:36,860
combat the next.

15
00:02:36,861 --> 00:02:42,780
During the war, Andy Rooney was a reporter for the Army newspaper, The Stars and Stripes.

16
00:02:42,781 --> 00:02:47,500
He was based in England, and he flew five combat missions with the bomber crews of the

17
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:49,500
Eighth Air Force.

18
00:02:49,501 --> 00:02:55,960
The men in the Eighth Air Force lived quite a civilized life on the base.

19
00:02:55,961 --> 00:02:58,680
They ate well, and they had entertainment.

20
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:01,000
They had good social facilities.

21
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:03,140
They could go into London and have a good time.

22
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:05,300
The next day, they died.

23
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,220
The infantry was in danger of death every day.

24
00:03:10,221 --> 00:03:15,840
They lived in the muck and the cold or the hot or whatever it was.

25
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:17,840
And there was no relief for them.

26
00:03:17,841 --> 00:03:21,720
But I always thought that emotionally,

27
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:28,260
8th Air Force crewmen went through more than the average infantryman.

28
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:37,120
The Air Force bombed Europe with B-24 Liberators and B-17 Flying Fortresses.

29
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,660
Each plane was rugged and well-suited for the job.

30
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:43,120
Each required a crew of 10 men.

31
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:52,300
On a B-17, five guys got the plane where it was supposed to go and do the job it was sent

32
00:03:52,301 --> 00:03:53,300
to do.

33
00:03:53,301 --> 00:03:59,880
A pilot, a co-pilot, a navigator, a radio operator, and a bombardier.

34
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:05,540
They sat in the front of the plane.

35
00:04:05,541 --> 00:04:08,680
Five more airmen protected the bomber on its perilous journey.

36
00:04:08,681 --> 00:04:16,380
A tail gunner, left and right waist gunners, a top turret gunner, and a ball turret gunner.

37
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:24,280
In a B-24, the ball turret could be safely retracted into the aircraft.

38
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:30,080
But in a B-17, the turret was permanently fixed to the bomber's belt.

39
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:37,720
All of the crewmen were exposed to enemy machine gun fire and anti-aircraft artillery.

40
00:04:37,721 --> 00:04:40,040
but it was the ball turret gunner,

41
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:43,300
particularly the man exposed beneath the B-17

42
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:44,820
with nowhere to hide,

43
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:47,280
that bore the brunt of the enemy's fury.

44
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:53,800
All of the ball turret gunners found their job frightening.

45
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:55,920
Many found it exciting.

46
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:58,860
And some found it suicidal.

47
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:05,860
For some people, it was the worst position ever.

48
00:05:05,861 --> 00:05:12,760
Some guys had said that if they were going to force me in there, I would refuse to fly.

49
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,020
Most people didn't like it because you're hanging outside of the airplane.

50
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:19,260
You can't see anybody on the crew.

51
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:23,180
The other people can see, the tail gunner can look back and see the waist gunners,

52
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,580
and waist gunners can look up through the bomb bay and see the pilot and co-pilot,

53
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:30,140
and the navigator and bombardier up front, but you're down there all by yourself.

54
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:32,460
It's kind of spooky down there.

55
00:05:32,461 --> 00:05:38,220
and it can make you nervous, just cooped up.

56
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:41,140
You can't move around or anything hardly in there.

57
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:42,560
It's pretty crowded.

58
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:49,220
It seemed that the sole most unlikely to escape a stricken B-17

59
00:05:49,221 --> 00:05:52,120
would be that lonely gunner in the ball turret.

60
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:57,700
The ball turret moved on geared tracks

61
00:05:57,701 --> 00:06:01,140
and there was one hatch at the top of the bubble.

62
00:06:01,141 --> 00:06:05,080
he got in just the right position and pressed the latch,

63
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,740
and he could climb out into the belly of the B-17.

64
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:10,740
However, what happened quite often

65
00:06:10,741 --> 00:06:15,000
was that the tracks on the bubble were hit

66
00:06:15,001 --> 00:06:16,640
so that he couldn't move it,

67
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,300
could not get it into position to climb out,

68
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:20,820
and was trapped in the bubble.

69
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:24,720
The one really terrible story,

70
00:06:24,721 --> 00:06:26,200
and I was present when it happened,

71
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:28,680
was that the gunner who was trapped

72
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:30,120
could not get out of the bubble,

73
00:06:30,121 --> 00:06:34,260
and the whole hydraulic system, the B-17, went out

74
00:06:34,261 --> 00:06:37,420
and the pilot could not get the landing gears down.

75
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:40,500
Here's this man trapped in the bubble.

76
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:43,900
The pilot had to make a belly landing.

77
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:47,540
He was crushed to death.

78
00:06:47,541 --> 00:07:01,541
the ball turret was a miserable place to fight a war but it had to be done the ball turret gunner

79
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:12,660
was the only person who could defend the bombers from below and in the monumental air battles that

80
00:07:12,661 --> 00:07:20,060
raged over Europe, there was no shortage of targets and no shortage of dangers facing

81
00:07:20,061 --> 00:07:21,360
the ball turret gunner.

82
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:30,680
In order to hit a moving fighter from a moving bomber, the gunner must aim his guns directly

83
00:07:30,681 --> 00:07:32,320
ahead of his intended target.

84
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,820
Ball turret gunners will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

85
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:41,040
We now return to Modern Marvels.

86
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:51,780
To survive the millstrom of aerial combat in World War II, an airman depended on two

87
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:58,820
things, the quality of his crew and the quality of his plane.

88
00:07:58,821 --> 00:08:09,140
The men of the 8th Air Force went to war in two types of heavy bombers, the B-24 Liberator,

89
00:08:09,141 --> 00:08:11,140
built by consolidated aircraft,

90
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:19,140
and the B-17 Flying Fortress, built by Boeing.

91
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:28,140
18,000 B-24s were produced compared to about 12,000 B-17s,

92
00:08:28,141 --> 00:08:32,140
but it was the B-17 that captured the imagination

93
00:08:32,141 --> 00:08:37,140
and came to symbolize the great struggle taking place over Europe.

94
00:08:37,141 --> 00:08:44,140
The B-17 was one of the great airplanes of all time.

95
00:08:44,141 --> 00:08:47,140
And like so many things, it's true sometimes of an athlete,

96
00:08:47,141 --> 00:08:50,140
you cannot put your finger on why he is so great.

97
00:08:50,141 --> 00:08:53,140
But he just has some quality about him.

98
00:08:53,141 --> 00:08:58,140
And the B-17 just had this quality about being great.

99
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:03,140
There has always been an argument between the B-24 people

100
00:09:03,141 --> 00:09:05,140
and the B-17 people.

101
00:09:05,141 --> 00:09:08,813
people and I was on the side of the B-17 people.

102
00:09:08,814 --> 00:09:12,180
If you had a contest with a lot of different

103
00:09:12,181 --> 00:09:16,406
categories, the B-24 would have won every category.

104
00:09:16,407 --> 00:09:19,140
But if I had to take my chance on

105
00:09:19,141 --> 00:09:23,860
going over Germany in a B-17 or a B-24, I'd take the B-17.

106
00:09:23,861 --> 00:09:36,620
the b-17 began life as the boeing model 299 rolled out on july 17th 1935 the new plane

107
00:09:36,621 --> 00:09:43,820
represented the state of the art in aircraft design the four-engine bomber could carry 2,500

108
00:09:43,821 --> 00:09:51,960
pounds of high explosives a range of 3,000 miles defended by streamlined machine gun blisters

109
00:09:51,961 --> 00:09:56,140
a Boeing press release called it an aerial battle cruiser.

110
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:02,220
A newspaper reporter marveled at the bomber's size and armament

111
00:10:02,221 --> 00:10:04,640
and dubbed it a flying fortress.

112
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,920
Boeing liked the sound of that, and the name stuck.

113
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:14,900
The Army saw the new bomber as a way of defending American coastlines

114
00:10:14,901 --> 00:10:17,380
from foreign invaders and enemy surface fleets.

115
00:10:17,381 --> 00:10:24,380
The big plane was also a perfect match for the Army's new top secret Norden bombsite.

116
00:10:24,381 --> 00:10:36,380
In 1936, the Army bought 13 of the bombers and changed the designation from Model 299 to YB-17.

117
00:10:36,381 --> 00:10:43,380
The Army bought more B-17s as Boeing continually improved the design.

118
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:53,080
In 1939, Boeing tried to improve the position of the belly gunner.

119
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:58,180
Instead of sitting above the old-style blister, the gunner now knelt in a bathtub.

120
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:03,080
But the uncomfortable position made it difficult to shoot accurately.

121
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:10,980
Boeing engineers replaced the belly bathtub with a new remote-control machine gun turret.

122
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:14,120
The gunner sat inside the plane.

123
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,060
He aimed and fired the guns with a special remote sight.

124
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:22,060
But they found a problem.

125
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:25,420
The awkward way that the belly gunner had to kneel

126
00:11:25,421 --> 00:11:27,080
and look through the aiming periscope

127
00:11:27,081 --> 00:11:29,400
induced severe vertigo and nausea.

128
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:33,680
So Boeing tried again.

129
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:36,380
They took out the remote-controlled turret

130
00:11:36,381 --> 00:11:38,120
and put in a new ball turret.

131
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:43,220
This spherical pod required that a man aim and fire the guns

132
00:11:43,221 --> 00:11:44,720
from inside the ball.

133
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:48,200
It seemed like a crazy idea.

134
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:52,080
The gunner had to hunch his body

135
00:11:52,081 --> 00:11:54,800
and draw his knees up to his chin to fit inside.

136
00:11:56,300 --> 00:11:59,080
The machine guns recoiled just inches from his head.

137
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:03,000
Hydraulics and electric motors powered the turret.

138
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:05,340
The gunner aimed his whole body,

139
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,700
working the controls with both hands and feet,

140
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:12,240
spinning, tilting, aiming, and firing.

141
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:21,580
In 1943, Wilbur Richardson was 20 years old when he volunteered for the Air Corps.

142
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,400
He wanted to become a pilot.

143
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:31,380
I had to take the first part of my physical, and the first thing they gave me was the eye test.

144
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:33,240
And they said I flumped it.

145
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:38,340
I couldn't tell distance, and I couldn't judge distances very well.

146
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:41,620
But they said I could go to gunnery school, though.

147
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:43,160
I was good enough for that.

148
00:12:43,220 --> 00:12:50,540
Richardson was packed off to an aerial gunnery school.

149
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:53,640
These schools popped up all over the United States

150
00:12:53,641 --> 00:12:56,080
as the need for skilled gunners grew rapidly.

151
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:02,180
Their lessons began on the skeet range

152
00:13:02,181 --> 00:13:03,900
with shotguns and clay pigeons.

153
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:10,540
Aerial gunnery came easily to men raised with firearms.

154
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,840
Dick Bowman was from Idaho.

155
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:16,180
and he had been a duck hunter since he was 12 years old.

156
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:19,160
They gave us a pump shotgun,

157
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:23,020
and we'd go around the track an eighth of a mile round

158
00:13:23,021 --> 00:13:26,360
shooting at clay pigeons, which was nothing but fun.

159
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:32,680
I honestly believe that the best training of all in gunnery school

160
00:13:32,681 --> 00:13:34,520
was the shotgun training.

161
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:39,200
Anyone who was good at shooting skeet was good at shooting airplanes.

162
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:41,200
It came out that way.

163
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:47,220
The men learned all about the .50 caliber machine gun,

164
00:13:47,221 --> 00:13:51,220
how to load it, how to clean it, how to take it apart

165
00:13:51,221 --> 00:13:55,220
and put it back together again. They practiced so often, they could

166
00:13:55,221 --> 00:13:59,220
actually do it blindfolded. They learned the physics

167
00:13:59,221 --> 00:14:03,220
of hitting a moving target. With simulators, they studied things

168
00:14:03,221 --> 00:14:07,220
like angle of attack, closing speed, and bullet drop.

169
00:14:07,221 --> 00:14:11,220
Their final test came in the air

170
00:14:11,221 --> 00:14:13,280
when they fired on towed targets.

171
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:19,440
After gunnery school, they went to phase training.

172
00:14:20,180 --> 00:14:23,220
They were signed to an aircraft and became part of a crew.

173
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:28,740
The pilot was in charge, and he decided which gunner would take which position.

174
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:33,260
John Hurd, a 20-year-old sergeant from California,

175
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:37,660
realized that when it came to the ball turret, size did matter.

176
00:14:37,661 --> 00:14:43,620
Now there was only two positions available when I joined the crew.

177
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:46,860
That was tail gunner and ball turret gunner.

178
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:50,700
And the other guy that was with me, he was a little bit bigger than I am.

179
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:53,700
So the pilot said for me to get in the ball.

180
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:59,280
So if I'm going to be a ball turret gunner, I'm going to do it and not worry about it.

181
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:05,900
I was 5'5", so it was almost a lead pipe cinch I was going in that ball turret.

182
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:14,420
in phase training the ten men learned to fight as one team they learned each

183
00:15:14,421 --> 00:15:19,640
other's strengths and weaknesses they learned to count on one another they had

184
00:15:19,641 --> 00:15:24,980
begun to forge the bonds that would see them through the war

185
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:30,940
Wilbur Richardson had originally joined his crew as the tail gunner after three

186
00:15:30,941 --> 00:15:35,220
long months of working together the last thing he wanted was to have one of the

187
00:15:35,221 --> 00:15:39,000
a crew replaced by a Rupple.

188
00:15:39,001 --> 00:15:41,560
Just before we were finishing up phrase training,

189
00:15:41,561 --> 00:15:47,460
my ball turret gunner came to me one day, and he said,

190
00:15:47,461 --> 00:15:52,740
you know, I'm thinking about changing crews,

191
00:15:52,741 --> 00:15:55,140
because I don't like the ball turret.

192
00:15:55,141 --> 00:15:58,640
And he said, if I don't get out of the ball turret,

193
00:15:58,641 --> 00:16:02,380
well, then I'll probably ask to go somewhere else.

194
00:16:02,381 --> 00:16:04,040
So we swapped positions.

195
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:09,700
I adjusted quickly and tried to make a game of it,

196
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:11,340
had fun as much as possible.

197
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:13,500
You have to make do with what you have,

198
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:16,500
and so I tried to look at it that way.

199
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:23,620
In three months, the air crews were deemed ready for combat.

200
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:25,840
They crossed the Atlantic for England,

201
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:29,480
eager to begin their assault on Hitler's thousand-year Reich.

202
00:16:29,481 --> 00:16:33,980
They had no idea what to expect.

203
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:42,500
In 1942, two B-17s were caught in an Arctic blizzard, forcing an emergency landing.

204
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:47,320
The bombers remained buried under 250 feet of ice in Greenland.

205
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:49,480
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

206
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:55,180
We now return to Modern Marvels.

207
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:06,480
The first American bomber crews arrived in England in the spring of 1942.

208
00:17:06,481 --> 00:17:17,480
They were the vanguard of the newly formed 8th Bomber Command, what came to be called the 8th Air Force.

209
00:17:17,481 --> 00:17:28,480
On August 17th, 1942, the 8th Air Force launched its first flying fortress strike of the war.

210
00:17:28,481 --> 00:17:40,720
A dozen B-17s crossed the English Channel to bomb a French rail yard full of Nazi munitions.

211
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:50,100
Surprisingly, the daylight raid was not a suicide mission.

212
00:17:50,820 --> 00:17:54,640
The target was smashed and only two B-17s suffered minor damage.

213
00:17:54,641 --> 00:18:03,500
sergeant kent west the ball turret gunner on a b-17 named the birmingham blitz creek

214
00:18:03,501 --> 00:18:10,260
caught a german fighter in his sights he became the first eighth air force gunner to shoot down

215
00:18:10,261 --> 00:18:11,540
a german aircraft

216
00:18:11,541 --> 00:18:21,820
the buildup of american forces continued into the spring of 1943

217
00:18:21,821 --> 00:18:24,920
as more bombers brought more airmen,

218
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:27,620
much to the dismay of many Brits,

219
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:30,600
who lamented, the Yanks are overpaid,

220
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:33,580
oversexed, and overhear.

221
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:40,680
The airmen lived on bases scattered across the English countryside.

222
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:43,480
They enjoyed many of the comforts of home,

223
00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:46,300
like warm beds and hot meals,

224
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:50,700
the kind of lifestyle that the average soldier would kill for.

225
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:57,820
But Frank Coleman, a ball turret gunner from Nevada, couldn't even give it away.

226
00:18:57,821 --> 00:19:06,020
We had a 40-hour pass, went down to London, we're in a bar, and one of these paratroopers

227
00:19:06,021 --> 00:19:09,940
had just come back, and they'd talk about, well, you guys go home and you've got a warm

228
00:19:09,941 --> 00:19:12,000
dinner and you've got a bed to sleep in and it's all great.

229
00:19:12,001 --> 00:19:14,000
So I'll trade you places.

230
00:19:14,001 --> 00:19:16,320
And I said, OK, I'll trade you places.

231
00:19:16,321 --> 00:19:17,320
And he says, you will?

232
00:19:17,321 --> 00:19:19,500
And I said, sure, I'll trade you places.

233
00:19:19,501 --> 00:19:20,500
He was a corporal.

234
00:19:20,501 --> 00:19:21,500
I was a staff sergeant.

235
00:19:21,501 --> 00:19:26,700
taking my blouse off he says you mean the ranka go with it and i says absolutely and as he started

236
00:19:26,701 --> 00:19:31,260
taking his boss off he's wait a minute where'd you fly i says the ball turned he says you go to hell

237
00:19:38,460 --> 00:19:44,620
the number of raids steadily increased by the summer of 43 the eighth air force was launching

238
00:19:44,621 --> 00:19:50,540
an attack every couple of days sometimes putting as many as two or three hundred bombers in the air

239
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:55,400
The sky, in the words of one Englishman, was never still.

240
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:01,980
To the folks back in the States, it looked like a fun way to fight a war.

241
00:20:03,240 --> 00:20:05,840
Glenn Miller entertained the troops with evening dances.

242
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,920
Jimmy Stewart became a bomber pilot.

243
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,280
Even Clark Gable joined the Air Force.

244
00:20:13,281 --> 00:20:23,180
the movie star went to england and made a motion picture about the bomber crews in action

245
00:20:23,181 --> 00:20:29,860
i remember i met him half a dozen times and i'd never seen a movie star before

246
00:20:29,861 --> 00:20:37,680
and i i still have the vision in my mind i can't get over how good clark gable looked

247
00:20:37,681 --> 00:20:41,154
in a uniform. I mean, everything fit just perfectly.

248
00:20:41,155 --> 00:20:43,760
I suppose he had tailors do it for him.

249
00:20:43,761 --> 00:20:52,060
But if you wanted the picture of the all-American Air Force officer, Clark Gable was it.

250
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:58,540
Hello, fellas. Well, how was it? I don't think those Germans like that.

251
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:07,120
Gable's film, called Combat America, was shown to audiences stateside and gave them an idea of

252
00:21:07,121 --> 00:21:08,760
what the gunners faced every day.

253
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:13,760
It wasn't as much fun as the folks back home had thought.

254
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:17,880
On average, during the first year of the bombing campaign,

255
00:21:18,380 --> 00:21:22,780
two out of three airmen did not survive their tour of duty with the 8th Air Force.

256
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:31,600
In July of 1943, the 8th Air Force got its first hero.

257
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:36,480
A B-17 had been hit on a raid over Europe and caught on fire.

258
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:43,340
The bald turret gunner, a sergeant with an unfortunate reputation, managed to save the day.

259
00:21:45,020 --> 00:21:48,020
Maynard Smith was his full name, but he was known as Snuffy Smith.

260
00:21:48,760 --> 00:21:51,940
And he really was a follow-up, to put it nicely.

261
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:55,600
Some of the best fighters were the worst soldiers.

262
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:58,560
That was true in both the Air Force and the infantry.

263
00:21:59,300 --> 00:22:02,980
The guys who just went their own way, and Snuffy Smith went his own way.

264
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:05,580
So one of the other men was hit,

265
00:22:05,980 --> 00:22:10,140
and it was lying, bleeding to death on the floor of the B-17.

266
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:13,700
And there was fire on board,

267
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:17,000
and Nuffy Smith just did everything,

268
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:21,660
saving this man's life and putting the fire out

269
00:22:21,661 --> 00:22:24,840
with the only liquid a man had available to him at that point.

270
00:22:25,740 --> 00:22:27,780
And he got back.

271
00:22:27,860 --> 00:22:30,780
Anyway, it was a story of great heroism on board a B-17.

272
00:22:30,781 --> 00:22:37,980
So they get back to the base, and the War Department was looking for a hero in the 8th Air Force.

273
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:42,600
So they chose to give Snuffy Smith the Medal of Honor.

274
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:55,340
And the Secretary of War arrives, and they usher him in, and everybody assembles, and the band's playing, and the flags are flying, and everybody's at attention.

275
00:22:55,660 --> 00:22:59,400
And they can't find Snuffy Smith to give him the Medal of Honor.

276
00:22:59,401 --> 00:23:09,400
Well, of all things, Snuffy Smith had screwed up again, and he'd been put on KP when the Secretary of War was out there waiting to pin a Medal of Honor on him.

277
00:23:09,401 --> 00:23:14,400
So they found him, got him dressed up, and gave him this Medal of Honor.

278
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:24,400
Snuffy Smith was the only ball-turret gunner and one of only three enlisted men in the 8th Air Force to receive the Medal of Honor.

279
00:23:24,401 --> 00:23:27,400
He flew only four more missions.

280
00:23:27,401 --> 00:23:31,400
The War Department sent him home to sell war bombs.

281
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:41,400
During the war, the British Royal Air Force bombed German targets at night.

282
00:23:41,401 --> 00:23:43,400
It was safer to bomb at night.

283
00:23:43,401 --> 00:23:48,400
But in the dark, the British bombers couldn't identify and strike a specific target.

284
00:23:48,401 --> 00:23:52,400
So they settled on a doctrine of carpet bombing.

285
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:58,880
The American Air Force, on the other hand, believed in precision bombing.

286
00:23:59,520 --> 00:24:03,740
Precision attacks could destroy German targets more efficiently than carpet bombing.

287
00:24:04,740 --> 00:24:09,680
The American bombers wanted to hit a specific target, and that meant they had to see the target.

288
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:13,320
And that meant they had to bomb during daylight.

289
00:24:13,321 --> 00:24:24,620
On one mission, the 8th Air Force sent 291 B-17s on a raid against the German factory in the heart of the Third Reich.

290
00:24:26,220 --> 00:24:28,320
The bombers came under heavy attack.

291
00:24:32,580 --> 00:24:38,460
In a long-running melee, German Messerschmitt and Folk Volf fighters destroyed 60 bombers.

292
00:24:43,320 --> 00:24:50,320
That meant 600 airmen were either dead or in enemy prison camps.

293
00:24:50,321 --> 00:24:59,320
That day, October 14, 1943, is forever remembered as Black Thursday.

294
00:24:59,321 --> 00:25:09,320
It was such a disaster that some questioned whether the Air Force should, or even could, continue its daylight campaign against Germany.

295
00:25:09,321 --> 00:25:17,460
The American air crews had to fly 25 combat missions, and then their tour of duty was done.

296
00:25:18,060 --> 00:25:27,320
But with the loss rates going up, it didn't take a mathematical genius to figure out that their chances of surviving their tour of duty were not that good.

297
00:25:27,321 --> 00:25:41,321
The B-17 is powered by four 1,200-horsepower Wright R-1820 radial piston engines.

298
00:25:42,500 --> 00:25:47,780
It has a range of 3,400 miles and a top speed of 302 miles per hour.

299
00:25:48,500 --> 00:25:51,360
Ball turret gunners will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

300
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:56,180
We now return to Modern Marvels.

301
00:25:57,320 --> 00:26:07,880
During World War II, a man could take just so much combat before he reached his breaking point.

302
00:26:09,180 --> 00:26:15,600
The Eighth Air Force knew that, so it came up with a rule that was supposed to keep men from cracking.

303
00:26:17,460 --> 00:26:23,440
The rule was, when you flew 25 missions, you were done. You could go home.

304
00:26:27,320 --> 00:26:32,420
Each time an airman took off, flew a combat mission, and returned to base,

305
00:26:32,421 --> 00:26:35,480
he was one step closer to that magic number.

306
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:39,960
On paper, 25 seemed reasonable, but in reality,

307
00:26:40,460 --> 00:26:44,480
the life expectancy of a bomber crew was just 15 missions.

308
00:26:51,300 --> 00:26:56,080
Each mission began the same way, long before sunrise.

309
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:59,220
They called us out about 3 o'clock.

310
00:26:59,760 --> 00:27:02,620
As gunners, we had to go down and get our guns,

311
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:05,140
clean them, take them out to the airplane.

312
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:10,020
And they gave us a few minutes, a little time to go to the mess hall,

313
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:12,260
but we usually didn't eat anything.

314
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:14,800
Most of us had maybe a few sips of coffee,

315
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:17,020
maybe half a piece of toast.

316
00:27:17,380 --> 00:27:18,480
That might be it.

317
00:27:22,380 --> 00:27:25,680
The gunners joined the rest of their crew in the big briefing room.

318
00:27:25,681 --> 00:27:31,060
They listened as their commander described the details of the mission and their target for today.

319
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:40,660
Remember when you approach the town what your indicated altitude is going to be.

320
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:47,460
Remember that you are not to go below that indicated altitude.

321
00:27:47,461 --> 00:27:55,440
Now, as you approach the area, you're going to run into rather heavy concentrations of

322
00:27:55,441 --> 00:27:56,440
flak.

323
00:27:56,441 --> 00:28:01,640
That flak has to be watched for.

324
00:28:01,641 --> 00:28:05,880
You've got to deal with it as you've been taught to deal with it.

325
00:28:05,881 --> 00:28:12,420
At the plane, the crews dressed and made a final check of their gear.

326
00:28:12,421 --> 00:28:13,960
Soon, they were airborne.

327
00:28:13,961 --> 00:28:27,961
their first step was to assemble in proper formation the planes were staggered side to

328
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:37,460
side and top to bottom in an arrangement called the combat box the box put each plane in a position

329
00:28:37,461 --> 00:28:40,600
where its guns could be concentrated to do the most damage.

330
00:28:42,340 --> 00:28:44,600
The bombers had to stay in this formation

331
00:28:44,601 --> 00:28:46,500
all the way to the target and back.

332
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:50,640
Holding this formation was their only chance of survival.

333
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,700
Once in formation, they headed out across the English Channel.

334
00:29:00,760 --> 00:29:04,580
That's when the gunners would get into their gun positions

335
00:29:04,581 --> 00:29:07,820
and test fire their guns,

336
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:09,860
make sure everything was going

337
00:29:09,861 --> 00:29:11,640
and you'd stay in your gun position

338
00:29:11,641 --> 00:29:14,980
from that point until you made it back

339
00:29:14,981 --> 00:29:17,080
over the channel coming home.

340
00:29:23,140 --> 00:29:24,640
Climbing to a high altitude,

341
00:29:25,240 --> 00:29:26,080
the air thins,

342
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:29,060
and the crew was forced to don oxygen masks.

343
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:33,480
You better shave clothes

344
00:29:33,481 --> 00:29:38,160
because if you don't shave before you go on that mission,

345
00:29:38,280 --> 00:29:39,620
you have a little stubble at all,

346
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:42,620
your face is going to be a little sore when you get back

347
00:29:42,621 --> 00:29:46,180
because you wear that oxygen mask and it rubs on you

348
00:29:46,181 --> 00:29:48,220
and it makes your face sting a little.

349
00:29:51,020 --> 00:29:53,000
As the formation climbed higher,

350
00:29:53,001 --> 00:29:57,380
the temperature outside and inside the unpressurized aircraft

351
00:29:57,381 --> 00:30:01,200
dropped quickly to 40, 50 degrees below zero.

352
00:30:01,201 --> 00:30:08,200
Electrically heated flying suits and heavy fleece lined flight jackets kept them from freezing.

353
00:30:08,201 --> 00:30:14,200
It gets cold over Europe at altitude.

354
00:30:14,201 --> 00:30:20,200
I used to get icicles on my eyebrows and ice in the oxygen mask.

355
00:30:20,201 --> 00:30:26,200
You could always tell who was an air crewman when you were overseas,

356
00:30:26,201 --> 00:30:30,100
You were overseas because you see these guys walking down the street.

357
00:30:30,660 --> 00:30:31,880
They looked like raccoons.

358
00:30:31,881 --> 00:30:33,980
It's right around where the oxygen mask is.

359
00:30:34,040 --> 00:30:35,660
You'd always have some frostbite.

360
00:30:36,580 --> 00:30:41,140
And so your faces have this red mark on it all the time.

361
00:30:44,280 --> 00:30:49,900
By 1944, the bombers began to enjoy the company of friendly fighter escorts.

362
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:54,700
The fighters protected them all the way to the target and back.

363
00:30:56,200 --> 00:31:03,200
If the German fighters attacked, the powerful thunderbolts and the mighty Mustangs would drive them away.

364
00:31:11,200 --> 00:31:18,200
But in the first years of the air war, in 1942 and 43, those long-range escort fighters were not available.

365
00:31:18,201 --> 00:31:22,200
The bombers were on their own.

366
00:31:22,201 --> 00:31:25,650
their own. The Germans had very fine airplanes.

367
00:31:25,651 --> 00:31:29,540
They could fly at the level that the bombers were at,

368
00:31:29,680 --> 00:31:33,086
30,000 feet. They were fast. They were heavily armed.

369
00:31:33,087 --> 00:31:35,720
They had excellent pilots, and they would

370
00:31:35,721 --> 00:31:41,640
come out, and they would shoot up the aircraft constantly while they were on the way to the target.

371
00:31:43,960 --> 00:31:49,760
It was up to the gunners to protect their planes. They constantly scanned the skies.

372
00:31:49,761 --> 00:31:52,800
They twisted and turned for any sign of the enemy,

373
00:31:53,340 --> 00:31:55,680
a move some called the Messerschmitt twist.

374
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:00,760
We were always instructed to keep the turrets moving.

375
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:04,780
Even the waste gunners were to move their gun once in a while

376
00:32:04,781 --> 00:32:10,080
so that the enemy fighters could see that the gunners were awake.

377
00:32:10,920 --> 00:32:15,940
And I developed a plan fairly quickly

378
00:32:15,941 --> 00:32:18,860
that if I saw an airplane out there

379
00:32:18,861 --> 00:32:21,560
and I knew it was an enemy fighter,

380
00:32:21,561 --> 00:32:24,600
I'd give him a short burst even though he might be a little

381
00:32:24,601 --> 00:32:29,140
farther out and a good shot would do him damage.

382
00:32:29,141 --> 00:32:30,900
Because I wanted him to know I was watching him.

383
00:32:34,700 --> 00:32:36,980
The first man to spot an enemy plane

384
00:32:36,981 --> 00:32:41,420
instantly alerted the rest of the crew through the intercoll.

385
00:32:41,421 --> 00:32:44,780
When you see it in movies, it's a waist gunner to pilot,

386
00:32:44,781 --> 00:32:47,060
or a pilot to the tail gunner, and all that.

387
00:32:47,061 --> 00:32:48,180
We didn't bother.

388
00:32:48,181 --> 00:32:50,860
You know, you knew each other's names, so I said,

389
00:32:51,160 --> 00:32:52,720
hey, Pete, what's going on back there?

390
00:32:54,640 --> 00:32:57,160
German fighters zoomed in from all directions.

391
00:32:57,980 --> 00:33:00,780
Each attack lasted only a few terrifying seconds,

392
00:33:01,660 --> 00:33:04,240
each riddling the bombers with machine gun bullets.

393
00:33:05,800 --> 00:33:09,340
If it's a nose-on attack, you get maybe one burst,

394
00:33:09,560 --> 00:33:12,980
two at the most, as they come by,

395
00:33:13,080 --> 00:33:14,460
because they're coming out too fast.

396
00:33:14,461 --> 00:33:19,540
and if they would come in and come in towards the side or towards the tail,

397
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:22,820
then you'd get three or four bursts out because they're a little slower.

398
00:33:23,860 --> 00:33:27,200
But at any of them, you don't see them very long.

399
00:33:27,920 --> 00:33:30,720
Three or four bursts, that's about all you get. They're gone.

400
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:36,360
While the gunners dealt with the fighters,

401
00:33:36,361 --> 00:33:39,660
the pilots held the bombers in formation as best they could.

402
00:33:40,280 --> 00:33:44,440
They couldn't take evasive action and risk going off course and missing the target.

403
00:33:44,460 --> 00:33:50,380
At the precise moment, the bombardier released the deadly cargo.

404
00:34:03,340 --> 00:34:08,420
On one bomb run, Wilbur Richardson's squadron was flying in the middle position.

405
00:34:09,340 --> 00:34:12,360
Another squadron was flying above his and another below.

406
00:34:12,361 --> 00:34:16,360
One B-17 in the lower squadron, the Miss Donna May,

407
00:34:16,361 --> 00:34:19,360
inexplicably drifted out of position.

408
00:34:19,361 --> 00:34:21,360
As the top squadron dropped its bombs,

409
00:34:21,361 --> 00:34:23,360
Richardson witnessed one of the most tragic

410
00:34:23,361 --> 00:34:26,360
and unforgettable accidents of the entire war.

411
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:33,360
The airplanes that were above us had dropped the bombs.

412
00:34:33,361 --> 00:34:35,360
The fellow that was under us dropped his.

413
00:34:35,361 --> 00:34:40,360
And just about then, this bomb went by our airplane,

414
00:34:40,361 --> 00:34:47,360
airplane and I watched that bomb hit the elevator of the airplane that was below us.

415
00:34:47,361 --> 00:34:50,360
And I watched it start straight down.

416
00:34:50,361 --> 00:34:53,360
It just immediately started going down.

417
00:34:53,361 --> 00:34:54,360
No other movement.

418
00:34:54,361 --> 00:34:56,360
It was just headed down.

419
00:34:56,361 --> 00:34:59,360
And he was going rather sharply.

420
00:34:59,361 --> 00:35:06,360
It didn't twist and turn, but it dropped so fast that no one got out of it.

421
00:35:06,361 --> 00:35:10,600
I'm sure if they could have, they would have, but no one ever got out.

422
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:17,900
At bombs away, the perilous mission wasn't over.

423
00:35:18,680 --> 00:35:23,740
They still had to maintain formation because more enemy fighters could be lurking in the clouds,

424
00:35:24,300 --> 00:35:27,500
waiting to attack the bombers as they made their way back to base.

425
00:35:27,501 --> 00:35:39,140
legendary dallas cowboys coach tom landry was a b-17 pilot for 30 combat missions with the

426
00:35:39,141 --> 00:35:44,720
eighth air force in europe he survived a crash landing that sheared off both wings of his bomber

427
00:35:44,721 --> 00:35:46,780
modern marvels return in a moment

428
00:35:46,781 --> 00:35:51,280
we now return to modern marvels

429
00:35:51,281 --> 00:35:58,100
when the heavy bombers

430
00:35:58,101 --> 00:36:01,400
B-17s and B-24s of the 8th Air Force

431
00:36:01,401 --> 00:36:03,460
weren't being hit by German fighters

432
00:36:03,461 --> 00:36:06,760
they were being blasted by German anti-aircraft guns

433
00:36:06,761 --> 00:36:15,920
their shells would explode at a predetermined altitude

434
00:36:15,921 --> 00:36:19,360
and fill the sky with razor sharp chunks of steel

435
00:36:19,361 --> 00:36:21,700
sort of like a giant hand grenade.

436
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:27,160
It was known to the airmen by the abbreviation of the German word

437
00:36:27,161 --> 00:36:30,920
Flieger Abwehrkönel, flack.

438
00:36:32,560 --> 00:36:33,900
Flack would scare you to death.

439
00:36:34,820 --> 00:36:37,500
And you looked at it, and you know you couldn't deviate on your course.

440
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:39,280
You knew you had to go through it.

441
00:36:39,900 --> 00:36:42,960
You'd sit there, how in the world are we going to make it through there?

442
00:36:42,961 --> 00:36:49,960
You just had to sit there and grin and bear it, you know, sort of.

443
00:36:49,961 --> 00:36:53,960
Swallow your heart sometimes.

444
00:36:53,961 --> 00:37:01,960
You got wounded at that altitude, you were in a lot of trouble because shock would set in.

445
00:37:01,961 --> 00:37:06,960
You might have five hours before you could get real medical attention.

446
00:37:06,961 --> 00:37:12,760
There were occasions when they felt an airman was so badly wounded, he'd never make it back.

447
00:37:13,120 --> 00:37:16,720
They'd just bundled him into a parachute and tossed him out of the airplane,

448
00:37:16,880 --> 00:37:21,840
hoping the Germans would give him medical treatment before they put him in a POW camp.

449
00:37:22,860 --> 00:37:26,320
You grow up rather quickly. You see all that stuff flying at you.

450
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:30,760
Hearing it come in the airplane or look up and see it coming through,

451
00:37:30,761 --> 00:37:36,060
you know that it's for real, and it's dangerous business.

452
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:42,040
I'm looking off to the side, and here's this B-17.

453
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:44,340
It's got a fire on the left wing.

454
00:37:45,020 --> 00:37:46,380
Some guys bailed out of it.

455
00:37:46,480 --> 00:37:47,700
I could see them dropping out.

456
00:37:48,300 --> 00:37:52,520
It just rolled over and spun on down

457
00:37:52,521 --> 00:37:56,120
and disappeared into the ground somewhere.

458
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:04,020
It was a terrible feeling to see these airplanes going,

459
00:38:04,021 --> 00:38:10,860
particularly when you might not see more than one or two parachutes come out, you know.

460
00:38:11,940 --> 00:38:13,800
That's the bad part about it.

461
00:38:14,520 --> 00:38:17,980
And it's a terrible thing for me to even talk about.

462
00:38:19,900 --> 00:38:25,340
You know, you'd watch another airplane go down, and you might even say, you know, it's better thee than me.

463
00:38:27,280 --> 00:38:30,160
And that sort of got you through it.

464
00:38:30,161 --> 00:38:36,520
And even though that's a terrible thing, I know, but that's the way it was.

465
00:38:39,160 --> 00:38:43,220
The terrible flack accounted for more losses than the German fighters.

466
00:38:44,460 --> 00:38:48,120
It's amazing that any planes made it through that curtain of steel.

467
00:38:49,460 --> 00:38:52,480
The survivors credit their safe return to a few things.

468
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:58,580
Good luck, a good crew, and an incredible aircraft.

469
00:38:58,581 --> 00:39:09,980
The job of that lonely gunner in the bald turret would have been more dangerous, more of a suicide mission, had it not been for the legendary stamina of the B-17.

470
00:39:12,740 --> 00:39:16,860
I give credit to the B-17 for bringing me back alive.

471
00:39:17,800 --> 00:39:25,640
There were occasions when any other airplane took hits the way it took, wouldn't have brought us back.

472
00:39:28,580 --> 00:39:30,340
God loved them.

473
00:39:32,680 --> 00:39:35,020
They'd bring you home when you didn't think you had a prayer.

474
00:39:35,720 --> 00:39:37,880
And they'd never let you down.

475
00:39:40,200 --> 00:39:44,480
When you see what the B-17 went through in combat

476
00:39:44,481 --> 00:39:49,040
and still make it back home, it was a miracle to me.

477
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:54,420
The men who returned counted their blessings,

478
00:39:55,060 --> 00:39:58,220
but they also counted the number of friends lost on the mission.

479
00:40:01,834 --> 00:40:12,707
One of the terrible experiences I had was going to their barracks where they lived, and their bunks were row on row, and they had little small private areas.

480
00:40:12,708 --> 00:40:23,580
They would set up pictures on their trunks, their army trunks, and they would have a picture of their girl back home or their wife or their mother or father.

481
00:40:23,581 --> 00:40:27,880
And the ball turret gunner would get killed.

482
00:40:27,980 --> 00:40:31,380
There is his bunk, next, in with these others,

483
00:40:31,480 --> 00:40:34,720
with all the pictures of his loved ones there.

484
00:40:34,820 --> 00:40:38,020
They come back, and he's gone.

485
00:40:38,120 --> 00:40:41,640
Somebody's got to pick this stuff up, you know, and move it.

486
00:40:41,740 --> 00:40:43,440
And then they move someone else in there.

487
00:40:43,540 --> 00:40:45,340
Yeah.

488
00:40:45,440 --> 00:40:48,040
I can almost see some of those faces still.

489
00:40:48,140 --> 00:40:50,140
You know, they were poker buddies.

490
00:40:50,240 --> 00:40:52,140
I mean, you played poker together.

491
00:40:53,580 --> 00:40:57,020
You can't forget faces.

492
00:40:59,020 --> 00:40:59,720
Hello.

493
00:41:03,560 --> 00:41:07,100
We talked as a crew, tried to be an encouragement to each other,

494
00:41:07,360 --> 00:41:09,340
and I think it was very supportive.

495
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:13,180
We had a chaplain sometimes that would come and talk to you if you needed it,

496
00:41:13,340 --> 00:41:15,480
because you did need it sometimes,

497
00:41:15,620 --> 00:41:18,440
because you just get so you just don't want to go no more.

498
00:41:18,441 --> 00:41:30,280
All too often they shed tears for their lost friends, and at times even for their foe.

499
00:41:30,281 --> 00:41:38,820
There was a pasture area there, farming area, and I'd just walk up the road past a clump

500
00:41:38,821 --> 00:41:39,820
of trees.

501
00:41:39,821 --> 00:41:41,820
Every time I come back for a mission

502
00:41:48,940 --> 00:41:54,320
I know I got there for about a half an hour just apologize

503
00:42:00,760 --> 00:42:04,700
Apologize to the Lord for what we did and that made it better

504
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:16,620
210,000 American airmen had flown into combat from their bases in England.

505
00:42:17,420 --> 00:42:19,520
26,000 never returned.

506
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:25,840
Wilbur Richardson was wounded in the leg by flack.

507
00:42:26,600 --> 00:42:28,360
Dick Bowman was hit in the chest.

508
00:42:29,620 --> 00:42:33,920
A chunk of flack went through Frank Coleman's turret and just missed his head by an inch.

509
00:42:34,900 --> 00:42:37,320
Bill Malloy had his share of close calls.

510
00:42:38,200 --> 00:42:42,480
John Hurd had to bail out of a burning B-17 on his 11th mission.

511
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:47,160
He was captured and spent the last year of the war as a POW.

512
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:56,380
I keep thinking at the time, and even now,

513
00:42:56,900 --> 00:42:59,100
even though it was as bad as it was,

514
00:42:59,101 --> 00:43:01,860
I felt better than being on the ground

515
00:43:01,861 --> 00:43:06,640
and riding in a tank or something like that.

516
00:43:07,320 --> 00:43:15,960
During the war, 12,371 B-17 flying fortresses were built.

517
00:43:16,660 --> 00:43:21,400
Almost 3,500, nearly a third, were shot down over Europe.

518
00:43:22,460 --> 00:43:28,280
The planes that survived the fighters and the flak have long since been consigned to the wrecking yard.

519
00:43:32,480 --> 00:43:34,000
All but a handful.

520
00:43:37,320 --> 00:43:46,140
Today, groups like the Arizona wing of the Confederate Air Force fly restored warbirds, like the B-17 Sentimental Journey.

521
00:43:49,620 --> 00:43:56,040
The men who flew in combat more than 50 years ago won't be around much longer.

522
00:43:56,041 --> 00:44:03,680
But with care, the plane's might,

523
00:44:05,340 --> 00:44:07,520
as airborne tributes to the courage

524
00:44:07,521 --> 00:44:09,980
and the sacrifices made by airmen

525
00:44:09,981 --> 00:44:12,200
like that lonely gunner in the ball turret

526
00:44:12,201 --> 00:44:14,120
so long ago.

